at line is not sunny.\n\nCheck points (n,1), (n-1,1), (n-2,1): all have y=1, horizontal line, slope 0, not sunny.\n\nCheck points (n,1), (n-1,2), (n-2,2): slope between (n,1) and (n-1,2) is -1, not allowed. So not sunny.\n\nCheck points (n,1), (n-2,2), (n-2,3): slope between (n,1) and (n-2,2) = (2-1)/(n-2 - n) = 1/(-2) = -1/2, which is allowed (sunny). Check if (n-2,3) lies on same line: slope between (n,1) and (n-2,3) = (3-1)/(n-2 - n) = 2/(-2) = -1, not equal to -1/2, so not collinear. So line through (n,1) and (n-2,2) has slope -1/2, passes through (n,1) and (n-2,2). Does it also pass through any other point in S? Let's check (n-1,2): slope between (n,1) and (n-1,2) = -1, not -1/2. So no. So that line covers exactly two points.\n\nCheck line through (n-1,1) and (n-2,3): slope = (3-1)/(n-2 - (n-1)) = 2/(-1) = -2, allowed. Does it pass through any other point? Check (n,1): slope (1-1)/(n - (n-1)) = 0, not -2. Check (n-2,2): slope (2-1)/(n-2 - (n-1)) = 1/(-1) = -1, not -2. So only two points.\n\nCheck line through (n-2,1) and (n-1,2): slope = (2-1)/(n-1 - (n-2)) = 1/1 = 1, allowed. Does it pass through any other point? Check (n,1): slope (1-1)/(n - (n-2)) = 0, not 1. Check (n-2,2): slope (2-1)/(n-2 - (n-2)) = infinite, not 1. So only two points.\n\nThus each of the three lines in the solution covers exactly two points. So with three sunny lines we can cover six points. With two sunny lines, each would need to cover at least three points. Let's see if any line can cover three points among S. Let's examine all possible triples.\n\nWe need to find three collinear points among S that are not on a vertical, horizontal, or slope -1 line. Let's list all points with coordinates:\n\n- A = (n,1)\n- B = (n-1,1)\n- C = (n-1,2)\n- D = (n-2,1)\n- E = (n-2,2)\n- F = (n-2,3)\n\nWe need to find triples among these that are collinear with slope not equal to 0,∞,-1.\n\nCheck triples:\n\n1. A, B, C: A-B horizontal? A and B have y=1, so line AB is horizontal (slope 0). Not allowed.\n\n2. A, B, D: A, B, D: A and B horizontal, D also y=1? D = (n-2,1). So A, B, D are all on y=1, horizontal, not allowed.\n\n3. A, B, E: A and B horizontal, E has y=2, not collinear.\n\n4. A, B, F: not collinear.\n\n5. A, C, D: A (n,1), C (n-1,2), D (n-2,1). Let's compute slopes: slope AC = (2-1)/(n-1 - n) = 1/(-1) = -1 (not allowed). So not allowed.\n\n6. A, C, E: A (n,1), C (n-1,2), E (n-2,2). Check collinearity: slope AC = -1, slope AE = (2-1)/(n-2 - n) = 1/(-2) = -1/2. Not equal, not collinear.\n\n7. A, C, F: slope AC = -1, slope AF = (3-1)/(n-2 - n) = 2/(-2) = -1, so A, C, F are collinear with slope -1. Not allowed.\n\n8. A, D, E: A (n,1), D (n-2,1), E (n-2,2). A-D horizontal, not collinear with E.\n\n9. A, D, F: A (n,1), D (n-2,1), F (n-2,3). A-D horizontal, not collinear.\n\n10. A, E, F: A (n,1), E (n-2,2), F (n-2,3). Check collinearity: slope AE = (2-1)/(n-2 - n) = 1/(-2) = -1/2. Slope AF = (3-1)/(n-2 - n) = 2/(-2) = -1. Not equal.\n\n11. B, C, D: B (n-1,1), C (n-1,2), D (n-2,1). B-C vertical, not allowed.\n\n12. B, C, E: B (n-1,1), C (n-1,2), E (n-2,2). B-C vertical, not collinear with E.\n\n13. B, C, F: B (n-1,1), C (n-1,2), F (n-2,3). B-C vertical, not collinear.\n\n14. B, D, E: B (n-1,1), D (n-2,1), E (n-2,2). B-D horizontal, not collinear with E.\n\n15. B, D, F: B (n-1,1), D (n-2,1), F (n-2,3). B-D horizontal, not collinear.\n\n16. B, E, F: B (n-1,1), E (n-2,2), F (n-2,3). Check collinearity: slope BE = (2-1)/(n-2 - (n-1)) = 1/(-1) = -1 (not allowed). So not allowed.\n\n17. C, D, E: C (n-1,2), D (n-2,1), E (n-2,2). C-D slope = (1-2)/(n-2 - (n-1)) = -1/(-1) = 1. C-E vertical? Actually C (n-1,2), E (n-2,2) have same y=2, horizontal slope 0. So not collinear.\n\n18. C, D, F: C (n-1,2), D (n-2,1), F (n-2,3). C-D slope = 1, C-F slope = (3-2)/(n-2 - (n-1)) = 1/(-1) = -1. Not collinear.\n\n19. C, E, F: C (n-1,2), E (n-2,2), F (n-2,3). C-E horizontal, not collinear with F.\n\n20. D, E, F: D (n-2,1), E (n-2,2), F (n-2,3) vertical line x=n-2, slope ∞, not allowed.\n\n