ver many points on a diagonal. Sunny lines cannot be parallel to those diagonals, so they can intersect each diagonal at at most one point. So the number of points on each diagonal is limited.\n\nThus we need to consider the diagonal lines x+y = d for d = 2,...,n+1. Each diagonal has d-1 points (since a+b = d). The non-sunny lines of slope -1 can cover all points on a diagonal if we use a line x+y = d (which is exactly the diagonal). So we can cover an entire diagonal with one non-sunny line. Sunny lines cannot be parallel to that diagonal, so they can intersect each diagonal at at most one point.\n\nThus the number of points on each diagonal that can be covered by sunny lines is at most k (since each sunny line can intersect each diagonal at most one point). Actually each sunny line can intersect each diagonal at at most one point, so across all sunny lines, we can cover at most k points per diagonal. The remaining points on each diagonal must be covered by non-sunny lines. Non-sunny lines can be vertical, horizontal, or slope -1. The slope -1 lines can cover entire diagonals. So we can use some slope -1 lines to cover entire diagonals.\n\nThus the bound on k may come from the fact that there are many diagonals, and we have limited number of non-sunny lines to cover them.\n\nLet's consider the number of diagonals: d runs from 2 to n+1 inclusive, so there are n diagonals. Each diagonal d has d-1 points.\n\nIf we have k sunny lines, each diagonal can have at most k points covered by sunny lines. So the total number of points covered by sunny lines across all diagonals is at most k * n (since there are n diagonals). But the total number of points is n(n+1)/2. So the number of points that must be covered by non-sunny lines is at least n(n+1)/2 - k*n = n*(n+1)/2 - k*n = n*( (n+1)/2 - k). So we need to cover that many points with n - k non-sunny lines.\n\nNow, each non-sunny line can cover many points if it's a slope -1 line (covers an entire diagonal). A vertical line c