has 2 points, two lines will have a point from column 3. Column 4 has 1 point, one line will have a point from column 4.\n\nThus each line will have between 2 and 4 points.\n\nWe need to assign points such that each line's y-values are distinct and sums are distinct.\n\nLet's try to assign systematically.\n\nWe have points:\n\n- Column 1: (1,1) sum=2 y=1; (1,2) sum=3 y=2; (1,3) sum=4 y=3; (1,4) sum=5 y=4.\n- Column 2: (2,1) sum=3 y=1; (2,2) sum=4 y=2; (2,3) sum=5 y=3.\n- Column 3: (3,1) sum=4 y=1; (3,2) sum=5 y=2.\n- Column 4: (4,1) sum=5 y=1.\n\nWe need to assign each point to one of four lines L1-L4.\n\nWe can think of this as a Latin rectangle problem: we need to assign each column's points to lines such that each line gets at most one point per column, and each line's y-values are distinct, and sums are distinct.\n\nWe can try to assign column 1 points to lines L1-L4 as:\n\n- L1: (1,1) sum=2 y=1\n- L2: (1,2) sum=3 y=2\n- L3: (1,3) sum=4 y=3\n- L4: (1,4) sum=5 y=4\n\nNow each line has sum and y as above.\n\nNow column 2 points: (2,1) sum=3 y=1; (2,2) sum=4 y=2; (2,3) sum=5 y=3.\n\nWe need to assign them to lines that don't have same sum or y.\n\n- L1 currently sum=2, y=1. So L1 cannot take (2,1) because y=1 conflict, cannot take (2,2) because sum=4 not present but y=2 not present, but column 2 is free. Actually L1 can take (2,2) sum=4 y=2, column 2 free, sum=4 not present, y=2 not present. So L1 can take (2,2). L1 can also take (2,3) sum=5 y=3, sum=5 not present, y=3 not present. So L1 could take either (2,2) or (2,3). But we need to assign each column 2 point to a distinct line, and each line can have at most one point from column 2.\n\n- L2 currently sum=3, y=2. So L2 cannot take (2,2) because sum=4 not present, y=2 conflict (y=2 already present). L2 cannot take (2,1) because sum=3 already present (L2 has sum=3 from (1,2)). L2 cannot take (2,3) because sum=5 not present, y=3 not present, column 2 free. So L2 can take (2,3).\n\n- L3 currently sum=4, y=3. So L3 c